x,y)$ at the point $(-1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}=⟨1,-\\sqrt{2}⟩?$"] [17592186266054 "Given that $$\n\\mathbf{F}=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\n$$ what is $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}?$"] [17592186266055 "Given that $\\mathbf{F}=\\operatorname{ln}(xy)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ what is $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}?$"] [17592186266056 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=2xy{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x{e}^{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+y{z}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ \nFind $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}.$"] [17592186266057 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=xz\\operatorname{cos}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+x{z}^{2}{e}^{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ \nEvaluate $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,0,-2).$"] [17592186266058 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=x(y{e}^{z}+\\operatorname{ln}y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+xy{e}^{z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+z(x+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ \nEvaluate $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,1,0).$"] [17592186266075 "Given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=x{e}^{yz}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{e}^{yz}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ what is $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}?$"] [17592186266076 "Given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=({y}^{2}+{e}^{x})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}(xy)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ what is the scalar curl of $\\mathbf{F}?$"] [17592186266078 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=({x}^{2}+xy)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{ln}(x+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},0).$"] [17592186266129 "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F},$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}(x+y)+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(\\operatorname{cos}(x+y)+x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"] [17592186266130 "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F},$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=({e}^{y}-y\\operatorname{sin}x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{cos}x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"] [17592186266131 "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F},$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={e}^{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(x{e}^{y}+2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"] [17592186266132 "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=(\\operatorname{ln}y+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(\\frac{x}{y}-{z}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$"] [17592186266133 "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=(2x+y{e}^{z})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(x{e}^{z}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(xy{e}^{z}-2z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$"] [17592186269008 "Let $\\mathbf{p}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ n\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{q}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that $\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}$, find the value of $n.$"] [17592186269204 "Let $\\mathbf{a}=2\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=-\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}.$ Determine $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$"] [17592186269253 "Let $\\mathbf{a}=4\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k}.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$"] [17592186271969 "Let $\\mathbf{a}=8\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=6\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{k}.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$"] [17592186272079 "Let $\\mathbf{u}=7\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-4\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{v}=-\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}.$ Find $\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}.$"] [17592186272141 "Let $\\mathbf{u}=3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{v}=-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k}.$ Determine $\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}.$"] [17592186272146 "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨0,1,6⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨13,0,0⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$"] [17592186272170 "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-3,5,9⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨2,8,-4⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$"] [17592186275231 "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf{r}(t)=7\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+9\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\frac{a(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+b(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*"] [17592186275232 "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf{r}(t)=5\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\frac{a(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+b(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*"] [17592186275233 "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf{r}(t)=9\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\frac{a(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+b(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*"] [17592186275256 "![](images/0d/0d2aa60719643235f5ae684d59607199731626e15bac40fd6b14d071c099ab4a.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186275259 "![](images/67/678ab825415e107bca9554d90e2ad5a4d8cc90a5ef68d817972ef98dfc165922.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186275327 "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}(2t),\\hspace{1em}y={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(t),\\hspace{2em}0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$"] [17592186278287 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3u+2v \\\\ u+3v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186278288 "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}uv \\\\ \\frac{v}{u}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186278291 "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u-v} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}(uv)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186278296 "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{2}-{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186278297 "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}}{2} \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186278298 "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{v}^{2}+uv \\\\ v-u\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186278300 "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}u \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi /6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186278369 "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 1\\},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A=\n$$"] [17592186278393 "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=xy+z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}2v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}$*"] [17592186278394 "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=\\sqrt{8}u.$*"] [17592186278395 "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}+3yz$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}.$*"] [17592186278411 "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(x+2y-z)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u+2v,\\hspace{0.222em}2v⟩\n$$\n\n \nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le u\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le v\\le 2\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=6.$*"] [17592186278412 "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}6xyz\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩\n$$\n\n \nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=\\sqrt{3}.$*"] [17592186278446 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨2x,\\hspace{0.222em}2y,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩,\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-uv,\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2}⟩,\\hspace{1em}(u,v)\\in D⊂{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨-2{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-4uv,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{u}^{2}⟩.$*"] [17592186278448 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨y+z,\\hspace{0.222em}x+z,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2v,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩.$*"] [17592186278449 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨3u{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-2\\le u\\le 0,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le v\\le 1\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-6uv-3{v}^{2}⟩.$*"] [17592186278450 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨3,\\hspace{0.222em}z,\\hspace{0.222em}y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}uv⟩\n$$\n\nfor $-1\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$"] [17592186278455 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}v-u,\\hspace{0.222em}-2uv⟩\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 2.$"] [17592186278459 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨-x-z,\\hspace{0.222em}x-y,\\hspace{0.222em}-z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}v-u,\\hspace{0.222em}-v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $-1\\le u\\le 0$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$"] [17592186278461 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}-3xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1-2u⟩\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$"] [17592186278462 "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{z}^{2}+y$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨-u,\\hspace{0.222em}-u,\\hspace{0.222em}-v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?"] [17592186278463 "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=-x-y-z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2}-{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?"] [17592186278464 "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=x-{y}^{2}z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨uv,\\hspace{0.222em}-u,\\hspace{0.222em}-{v}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?"] [17592186278470 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩,\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}+2v,\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{3}+3v⟩,\\hspace{1em}(u,v)\\in D⊂{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}a{u}^{2}+bu+c\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$"] [17592186278471 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨x-\\frac{1}{z},\\hspace{0.222em}y+\\frac{1}{z},\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩,\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u{e}^{v},\\hspace{0.222em}u{e}^{-v},\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩,\\hspace{1em}(u,v)\\in D⊂{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}a{e}^{v}+b{e}^{-v}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$"] [17592186278478 "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=xy{z}^{2},$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=y-2x$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $-1\\le y\\le 1.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$${∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\boxed{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?"] [17592186278479 "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2}xyz,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-\\sqrt{2}x-\\sqrt{2}y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$${∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?"] [17592186278481 "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{z}^{2},$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$${∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?"] [17592186278495 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\hspace{0.222em}-y,\\hspace{0.222em}2x⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=3x-2y$ for $0\\le x\\le 5$ and $-3\\le y\\le 3.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?"] [17592186278506 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨-x,\\hspace{0.222em}-y,\\hspace{0.222em}xyz⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=2x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$"] [17592186278507 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨{z}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}x⟩,\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩,\\hspace{1em}(u,v)\\in D⊂{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2u⟩.$*"] [17592186278509 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨xy,\\hspace{0.222em}x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{z}⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le x.$"] [17592186278510 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y-z,\\hspace{0.222em}x+y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=x-2y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $-1\\le y\\le 1.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?"] [17592186278512 "For a surface $S$ given by $x=k(y,z)$ for $(y,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(P+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\frac{∂k}{∂y}-R\\frac{∂k}{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?"] [17592186278515 "For a surface $S$ given by $y=-{x}^{2}z$ for $(x,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\hspace{0.167em}xz+Q+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\hspace{0.167em}{x}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?\n\n*Hint: The orientation of $S$ should be chosen such that the unit normal vector has a positive  $\\mathbf{j}$-component.*"] [17592186278516 "For a surface $S$ given by $x=2yz$ for $(y,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(P+\\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\hspace{0.167em}z+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\hspace{0.167em}y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?"] [17592186278518 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\hspace{0.222em}y+2z,\\hspace{0.222em}2x-y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=y-x$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le y\\le 2.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?"] [17592186278519 "For a surface $S$ given by $x=k(y,z)$ for $(y,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(P-\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}-R\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?"] [17592186278522 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)$ for $-\\pi \\le x\\le 0$ and $-x\\le y\\le 0.$"] [17592186278545 "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨y,\\hspace{0.222em}x+z,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z={e}^{x}$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$"] [17592186278546 "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(xy),\\hspace{0.222em}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(xy),\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(xy)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $-x\\le y\\le 0.$"] [17592186278547 "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\hspace{0.222em}{z}^{2}+1⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=2\\sqrt{xy}$ for $3\\le x\\le 4$ and $1\\le y\\le 2.$"]}
